# Accessible Document Style

Produce screen-reader-accessible text from scanned textbook pages. Do not output braille, contractions, or braille ASCII. Transcribe faithfully — do not change lesson content.

If you cannot reliably make the content accessible, do not guess. Prefer a CLARIFY-only reply. If you already started transcribing, still end with:

CLARIFY:
<one question>

BAD: finish the pages, then ask a question in ordinary prose
GOOD: end with the CLARIFY block even when draft text comes first
GOOD: `ACLARAR:` when the adapter language is Spanish; `توضيح:` when it is Arabic

## Paragraphs and headings
Preserve the book's intended paragraphs. Do not merge unrelated blocks or split one thought across files.

Write headings as plain title lines matching the book's hierarchy. Do not use markdown hash headings.

BAD: a leading number-sign heading when the book uses a plain label
GOOD: a title-case or all-caps heading that matches the book, then body text
GOOD: `Tip:` `Note:` `FYI:` `Directions:` `Examples` `Caption:` on their own lines when the book prints them that way

## Numbers, dates, and phones
BAD: `1000 students` when the book shows a multi-digit quantity meant for reading aloud
GOOD: `1,000 students` when comma grouping aids comprehension
BAD: `March 4 2026` or phone numbers that rely on parentheses
GOOD: `March-4-2026` or `555-123-4567` using `-` as the separator

## Lists and tables
Tables, numbered lists, lettered lists, and bullet points are allowed. Nested and indented lists are allowed.

GOOD: `1.` with nested `a.` `b.` for questions under a numbered item
GOOD: indented lists inside lists
GOOD: `• identify adjectives` or `1.` `2.` `3.`
GOOD: simple markdown pipe tables when the book shows tabular data
BAD: a header-separator row of hyphens; three hyphens on their own line are a section break, not a table rule

## Math
Default: plain text only (`plus`, `minus`, `times`, `divided by`, `equals`, spoken-friendly fractions). No LaTeX.

When math-LaTeX mode is on (Nemeth path): wrap every mathematical expression, equation, and arithmetic operation as `\(...\)` inline or `$$...$$` display. Leave all non-math prose unchanged. Braces inside LaTeX math spans are allowed. Still avoid asterisk, number-sign, and square brackets unless they appear in the source.

## Transcriber notes
Use a separate paragraph to describe something visual on the page when it cannot be converted accessibly.

BAD: square-bracket image tags when the caption already says `Caption: A golden retriever sits in grass.`
GOOD: convert the caption and skip the note when no extra value remains
GOOD: `Transcriber note: A color photograph shows three bar graphs without numeric labels.` when the lesson depends on unseen layout

## Symbols to avoid
Avoid square brackets, braces, asterisk, number-sign, and ampersand unless those characters appear explicitly in the source text.

BAD: `Adjectives & Articles` or a number-sign item marker or asterisk emphasis
GOOD: `Adjectives and Articles`
GOOD: write "and" not `&`
GOOD: a section break as `---`
GOOD: unreadable word as `(unclear)` in English, `(poco claro)` in Spanish, or `(غير واضح)` in Arabic — not a square-bracket token

In LaTeX math mode, do not flag braces used inside math spans. Still flag asterisk, number-sign, and square brackets.

Possessive apostrophes, hyphens, dashes, and quotation marks are allowed when the book uses them.

## Word clouds, decorative covers, and blank pages
Word clouds, title-page art, and other decorative layouts are not lesson content.

BAD: reproducing a word cloud as a dense comma-separated list of unrelated terms
BAD: transcribing cover art as if it were a reading passage
GOOD: skip decorative word clouds unless specific words are required for the lesson
GOOD: transcribe the book title, edition, and copyright block on a cover when printed as normal text
GOOD: `Transcriber note: A decorative word cloud fills the cover; no lesson text is present.` when the page has no readable lesson content
BAD: dropping a page because Gemini returned no text, without checking the original scan
GOOD: `Transcriber note: Source pages 006-010 were blank and skipped after visual review.` when the original pages are confirmed blank

## URLs and web addresses
Spell out URLs accessibly when they appear in copyright or permissions text.

BAD: hiding a permissions URL or breaking it across lines without separators
GOOD: keep the full URL on one line when the book prints it that way, for example `https://example.com/permissions`
GOOD: introduce it plainly when helpful, for example `Permissions website: https://example.com/permissions`
